In a rectangular dissection of a square into \(R\) rectangles, the maximal number of distinct lines determined by the sides of the rectangles is given by \(R + 3\). This result holds because, for any such dissection, the sum of the number of distinct horizontal lines (\(H\)) and vertical lines (\(V\)) satisfies \(H + V = R + 3\). This equality is achievable, for example, by dividing the square into \(R\) vertical strips, which gives \(H = 2\) (top and bottom boundaries) and \(V = R + 1\) (left boundary, \(R-1\) cuts, and right boundary), so \(H + V = 2 + (R + 1) = R + 3\).

Attempts to exceed this sum, such as by adding more lines or creating more complex dissections, result in an increase in the number of rectangles or fail to utilize all lines, maintaining the bound \(R + 3\). For \(R = 2022\), the maximal number of distinct lines is therefore \(2022 + 3 = 2025\).

\boxed{2025}

